 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the not-for-profit corporation law, in relation to requiring\nconsent prior to the merger of cemetery corporations",
    "basePrintNo": "S7155",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7155-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 63,
       "fullName": "Timothy M. Kennedy",
       "imgName": "429_timothy_m._kennedy.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 429,
       "sessionMemberId": 1205,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "KENNEDY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1506, add §1506-d, N-PC L",
    "lawSection": "Not-for-Profit Corporation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7155\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the not-for-profit corporation law, in relation to\nrequiring consent prior to the merger of cemetery corporations\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo set a clear, rational and supportive state policy concerning mergers\nof cemetery corporations, with particular emphasis on (1) validating all\nprevious mergers; and (2) authorizing and encouraging pending and future\nmergers to avoid costly cemetery abandonments, that have already cost\nlocal governments throughout our state millions of dollars.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the not-for-profit corporation law to allow\ncemetery mergers subject to certain conditions.\n\nSection 2 of the bill makes it clear that state policy does not prohibit\ncemetery mergers and all mergers undertaken prior to January 1st, 2020,\nwhether approved by the State Cemetery Board or not, shall be valid and\nratified and not subject to the new proposals contained in this bill.\n\nSection 3 amends a relevant section of the not-for-profit corporation\nlaw to make clear that a cemetery corporation may acquire land for the\npurpose of a merger.\n\nSection 4 of the bill adds a new section of law that spells out the\nCemetery Merger Authorization process including setting standards for\nmergers of cemeteries that are within a 50 mile radius of each other and\nthose that are more than 50 miles apart. In either case, the proposed\nmerging entities must demonstrate and provide certain criteria and\ninformation to the State Cemetery Board including sufficiency of assets\nto maintain the properties and operations and in the case of mergers\noutside of a 50 mile radius, the following must be provided: and outline\nof financial management controls; an outline of total financial assets\nand demonstration of sufficiency of resources; information concerning\nthe location and access to all records; a plan for providing remote\nmaintenance; contact information for the \"surviving\" cemetery; proce-\ndures for conducting meetings of/at the merged cemetery.\n\nSection 5 of the bill provides that this act shall take effect imme-\ndiately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe cemetery abandonment crisis in New York State is reaching a true\nemergency stage-with our local governments on the receiving end of\nrecord numbers of cemetery abandonments despite efforts by the State\nLegislature to provide policy solutions. Cemetery abandonments can cost\nlocal governments millions of dollars, as evidenced by the Whispering\nPines abandonment, which has cost $2 million and the abandonments in St.\nLawrence County, which will soon cost over $2 million as well.\n\nOne of the tools used for many decades to avoid cemetery abonnement is\nmergers, where a larger and more financially stable cemetery would merge\nwith smaller entities, in an arrangement that was beneficial for the\nstate, local governments and the families of those who have loved ones\ninterred in the respective cemeteries. However, despite many decades of\nprecedent, the State Cemetery Board opined in late 2019 that there is\nnothing in state law that requires or authorizes them to approve merg-\ners, despite many decades of actually doing so.\n\nThis reversal of policy by the State Cemetery Board comes at a tremen-\ndously inopportune time when mergers are needed to avoid costly abandon-\nment. Thus, this bill sets a clear and rational state standard that will\nrecognize all previous mergers as legitimate and set up a process where-\nby pending and future mergers may be actualized.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nIf successfully implemented, significant savings to local governments\nmay occur to the extent that will no longer be financially liable for\nabandoned cemeteries.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill shall take effect immediately.\n",
